{\rtf1\ansi \ansicpg1251\stshfloch0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green102\blue204;\red0\green153\blue77;\red0\green102\blue178;}{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\froman\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\froman\fcharset204 Times New Roman;}{\f3\fswiss\fcharset204 Segoe UI;}{\f4\fswiss\fcharset0 Segoe UI;}{\f5\fswiss\fcharset0 Segoe UI;}{\f6\froman\fcharset0 Georgia;}{\f7\froman\fcharset0 Georgia;}{\f8\froman\fcharset204 Georgia;}}{\stylesheet {\ql Normal;}{\s1\sbasedon0\qc\sl-442\slmult0 Style1;}{\s2\sbasedon0\qc\sl-288\slmult0 Style2;}{\s3\sbasedon0\qj\fi350\sl-289\slmult0 Style3;}{\s4\sbasedon0\ql Style4;}{\s5\sbasedon0\ql Style5;}{\s6\sbasedon0\qj\sl-219\slmult0 Style6;}{\s7\sbasedon0\ql\sl-294\slmult0 Style7;}{\s8\sbasedon0\qj\sl-288\slmult0 Style8;}{\s9\sbasedon0\ql\fi-134\sl-288\slmult0 Style9;}{\s10\sbasedon0\ql Style10;}{\s11\sbasedon0\ql Style11;}{\s12\sbasedon0\ql Style12;}{\s13\sbasedon0\ql Style13;}{\s14\sbasedon0\ql Style14;}{\s15\sbasedon0\ql\fi-144\sl-293\slmult0 Style15;}{\s16\sbasedon0\ql Style16;}{\*\cs17\additive Default Paragraph Font;}{\*\cs18\sbasedon17\additive\cf1\f2\fs34 Font Style18;}{\*\cs19\sbasedon17\additive\expndtw10\cf1\f2\fs16 Font Style19;}{\*\cs20\sbasedon17\additive\b\cf1\f2\fs28 Font Style20;}{\*\cs21\sbasedon17\additive\cf1\f2\fs16 Font Style21;}{\*\cs22\sbasedon17\additive\i\expndtw10\cf1\f2\fs20 Font Style22;}{\*\cs23\sbasedon17\additive\i\expndtw20\cf1\f8\fs16 Font Style23;}{\*\cs24\sbasedon17\additive\scaps\expndtw20\cf1\f2\fs18 Font Style24;}{\*\cs25\sbasedon17\additive\cf1\f2\fs18 Font Style25;}{\*\cs26\sbasedon17\additive\cf1\f2\fs18 Font Style26;}{\*\cs27\sbasedon17\additive\expndtw20\cf1\f2\fs20 Font Style27;}{\*\cs28\sbasedon17\additive\cf1\f3\fs20 Font Style28;}{\*\cs29\sbasedon17\additive\b\cf1\f2\fs20 Font Style29;}{\*\cs30\sbasedon17\additive\cf1\f2\fs20 Font Style30;}{\*\cs31\sbasedon17\additive\ul\cf9 Hyperlink;}}\fet2{\*\ftnsep {\chftnsep\par }}{\*\aftnsep {\chftnsep\par }}\lnbrkrule\splytwnine\lyttblrtgr {\info{\title New Features of Latin Dances: Analysis of Salsa, ChaCha, and Rumba}{\author Jean-Philippe Aumasson, Simon Fischer, Shahram Khazaei, Willi Meier, Christian Rechberger}{\keywords Cryptanalysis, Stream Cipher, eSTREAM, Salsa20, ChaCha, Rumba}}\expshrtn\widowctrl\paperw11905\paperh16837\margl1933\margr1913\margt1699\margb1440 \sectd \ltrsect\sbknone\pgwsxn11905\pghsxn16837\marglsxn1933\margrsxn1913\margtsxn1699\margbsxn1440\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s1\qc\li1262\fi0\ri1262\sb0\sa0\sl-442\slmult0 \cs18\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs34 New Features of Latin Dances: \cs20\b\fs28 Analysis of Salsa, ChaCha, and Rumba\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s2\qc\li826\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb182\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 Jean-Philippe Aumasson\super 1\nosupersub , Simon Fischer\super 1\nosupersub , Shahram Khazaei\super 2\nosupersub , Willi Meier\super 1\nosupersub , and Christian Rechberger\super 3\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s4\qc\li0\fi0\ri5\sb182\sa0\sl-221\slmult0\tx163 \cs21\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs16 1\nosupersub {\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\lang1033 FHNW, Windisch, Switzerland\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s4\qc\li0\fi0\ri10\sb0\sa0\sl-221\slmult0\tx125 \cs21\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs16 2\nosupersub {\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\lang1033 EPFL, Lausanne, Switzerland\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s6\qc\li0\fi0\ri5\sb0\sa0\sl-221\slmult0 \cs21\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs16 3\nosupersub\lang1033  IAIK, Graz, Austria\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s6\qj\li571\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb82\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \cs19\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs16 Abstract. \cs21\expndtw0 The stream cipher Salsa20 was introduced by Bernstein in\lang1049  2005\lang1033  as a can\-didate in the eSTREAM project, accompanied by the reduced versions Salsa20/8 and Salsa20/12. ChaCha is a variant of Salsa20 aiming at bringing better diffusion for sim\-ilar performance. Variants of Salsa20 with up to\lang1049  7\lang1033  rounds (instead of\lang1049  20)\lang1033  have been broken by differential cryptanalysis, while ChaCha has not been analyzed yet. We in\-troduce a novel method for differential cryptanalysis of Salsa20 and ChaCha, inspired by correlation attacks and related to the notion of neutral bits. This is the first appli\-cation of neutral bits in stream cipher cryptanalysis. It allows us to break the 256-bit version of Salsa20/8, to bring faster attacks on the 7-round variant, and to break\lang1049  6-\lang1033  and 7-round ChaCha. In a second part, we analyze the compression function Rumba, built as the XOR of four Salsa20 instances and returning a 512-bit output. We find collision and preimage attacks for two simplified variants, then we discuss differential attacks on the original version, and exploit a high-probability differential to reduce complexity of collision search from 2\super 256\nosupersub  to 2\super 79\nosupersub  for 3-round Rumba. To prove the correctness of our approach we provide examples of collisions and near-collisions on simplified versions.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s1\ql\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb202\sa0 \cs20\i0\strike0\nosupersub\b\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs28 1\lang1033  Introduction\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 Salsa20\lang1049  [\cf10 5\cf1 ]\lang1033  is a stream cipher introduced by Bernstein in\lang1049  2005\lang1033  as a candidate in the eSTREAM project\lang1049  [\cf10 12\cf1 ],\lang1033  that has been selected in April\lang1049  2007\lang1033  for the third and ultimate phase of the competition. Three independent cryptanalyses were published\lang1049  [\cf10 11\cf1 ,\cf10 13\cf1 ,\cf10 16\cf1 ],\lang1033  reporting key-recovery attacks for reduced versions with up to\lang1049  7\lang1033  rounds, while Salsa20 has a total of\lang1049  20\lang1033  rounds. Bernstein also submitted to public evaluation the\lang1049  8-\lang1033  and 12-round variants Salsa20/8 and Salsa20/12\lang1049  [\cf10 6\cf1 ],\lang1033  though they are not formal eSTREAM candidates. Later he introduced ChaCha\lang1049  [\cf10 4\cf1 ,\cf10 3\cf1 ,\cf10 8\cf1 ],\lang1033  a variant of Salsa20 that aims at bringing faster diffusion without slowing down encryption.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s3\qj\li0\fi350\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 The compression function Rumba [\cf10 7\cf1 ] was presented in 2007 in the context of a study of generalized birthday attacks [\cf10 17\cf1 ] applied to incremental hashing [\cf10 2\cf1 ], as the component of a hypothetical iterated hashing scheme. Rumba maps a 1536-bit value to a 512-bit (intermediate) digest, and Bernstein only conjectures collision resistance for this function, letting a further convenient operating mode provide extra security properties as pseudo-randomness.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb101\sa0\sl-283\slmult0 \cs29\i0\strike0\nosupersub\b\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 Related Work. \cs30\b0 Variants of Salsa20 up to\lang1049  7\lang1033  rounds have been broken by dif\-ferential cryptanalysis, exploiting a truncated differential over\lang1049  3\lang1033  or\lang1049  4\lang1033  rounds. The knowledge of less than\lang1049  256\lang1033  key bits can be sufficient for observing a dif\-ference in the state after three or four rounds, given a block of keystream of\par \sect\sectd \ltrsect\sbkpage\pgwsxn11905\pghsxn16837\marglsxn1928\margrsxn1918\margtsxn1829\margbsxn1440\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 up to seven rounds of Salsa20. In\lang1049  2005,\lang1033  Crowley\lang1049  [\cf10 11\cf1 ]\lang1033  reported a 3-round differ\-ential, and built upon this an attack on Salsa20/5 within claimed 2\super 165\nosupersub  trials. In\lang1049  2006,\lang1033  Fischer et al.\lang1049  [\cf10 13\cf1 ]\lang1033  exploited a 4-round differential to attack Salsa20/6 within claimed 2\super 177\nosupersub  trials. In\lang1049  2007,\lang1033  Tsunoo et al.\lang1049  [\cf10 16\cf1 ]\lang1033  attacked Salsa20/7 within about 2\super 190\nosupersub  trials, still exploiting a 4-round differential, and also claimed a break of Salsa20/8. However, the latter attack is effectively slower than brute force, cf.\lang1049  \f2\cchs204 \'a7\cf11\f2\cchs204 3.5\cf1 .\lang1033  Tsunoo et al. notably improve from previous attacks by reducing the guesses to certain bits\f2\cchs204\lang1049 \'97\f0\cchs0\lang1033 rather than guessing whole key words\f2\cchs204\lang1049 \'97\f0\cchs0\lang1033 using nonlinear approximation of integer addition. Eventually, no attack on ChaCha or Rumba has been published so far.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb187\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs29\i0\strike0\nosupersub\b\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 Contribution. \cs30\b0\expndtw-20 We\expndtw0  introduce a novel method for attacking Salsa20 and ChaCha (and potentially other ciphers) inspired from correlation attacks, and from the notion of neutral bit, introduced by Biham and Chen\lang1049  [\cf10 9\cf1 ]\lang1033  for attacking SHA-0. More precisely, \expndtw-20 we\expndtw0  use an empirical measure of the correlation between certain key bits of the state and the bias observed after working a few rounds backward, in order to split key bits into two subsets: the extremely relevant key bits to be subjected to an exhaustive search and filtered by observations of a biased output-difference value,and the less significant key bits ultimately determined by exhaustive search. \expndtw-20 To\expndtw0  the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that neutral bits are used for the analysis of stream ciphers. Our results are summa\-rized in Tab.\lang1049  \cf11 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "bookmark0"}}{\fldrslt 1}}\cf1 .\lang1033  \expndtw-20 We\expndtw0  present the first key-recovery attack for the 256-bit version of Salsa20/8, improve the previous attack on 7-round Salsa20 by a factor 2\super 39\nosupersub , and present attacks on ChaCha up to 7 rounds. The 128-bit versions are also investigated. In a second part, we first show collision and preimage attacks for simplified versions of Rumba, then we present a differential analysis of the orig\-inal version using the methods of linearization and neutral bits: our main result is a collision attack for 3-round Rumba running in about 2\super 79\nosupersub  trials (compared to 2\super 256\nosupersub  with a birthday attack). \expndtw-20 We\expndtw0  also give examples of near-collisions over three and four rounds.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb38\sa0 \cs29\i0\strike0\nosupersub\b\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 {\*\bkmkstart bookmark0}T{\*\bkmkend bookmark0}able\lang1049  1.\lang1033  \cs30\b0 Complexity of the best attacks known, with success probability\lang1049  1/2.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s6\ql\li2347\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb72\sa0 \cs21\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs16 Salsa20/7 Salsa20/8 ChaCha6 ChaCha7 Rumba3\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s6\qc\li0\fi0\ri197\sb62\sa0\sl-216\slmult0\tx1637\tx2458\tx3259 \cs21\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs16 Before\lang1049      2"\f2\cchs204 \'b0\f2\cchs204 {\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }2^{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }2^{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }2^\lang1033  2\super 266\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s6\qc\li0\fi0\ri115\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0\tx1483\tx2304\tx3106 \cs21\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs16 Now     2\super 151\nosupersub {\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }2\super 251\nosupersub {\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }2\super 139\nosupersub {\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }2\super 248\nosupersub  2\super 79\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb82\sa0\sl-283\slmult0 \cs29\i0\strike0\nosupersub\b\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 Road Map. \cs30\b0\expndtw-20 We\expndtw0  first recall the definitions of Salsa20, ChaCha, and Rumba in \f0\cchs0 \'a7\f0\cchs0 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "bookmark1"}}{\fldrslt  \cf11 2}}, then \f0\cchs0 \'a7\f0\cchs0  \cf11 3 \cf1 describes our attacks on Salsa20 and ChaCha, and \f0\cchs0 \'a7\f0\cchs0  \cf11 4 \cf1 presents our cryptanalysis of Rumba. The appendices give the sets of constant values, and some parameters necessary to reproduce our attacks.\par \sect\sectd \ltrsect\sbkpage\pgwsxn11905\pghsxn16837\marglsxn1928\margrsxn1481\margtsxn1805\margbsxn1440\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s1\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \cs28\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs20 {\*\bkmkstart bookmark1}2{\*\bkmkend bookmark1}\lang1033    \cs20\b\f0\cchs0\fs28 Specification of Primitives\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb24\sa0\sl-293\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 In this section, we give a concise description of the stream ciphers Salsa20 and ChaCha, and of the compression function Rumba.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s10\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb197\sa0 \cs29\i0\strike0\nosupersub\b\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs20 2.1\lang1033  Salsa20\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri427\sb202\sa197\sl-288\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 The stream cipher Salsa20 operates on 32-bit words, takes as input a 256-bit key k\lang1049  =\lang1033  (k\sub o\nosupersub , \cs23\i\expndtw20\f6\cchs0\fs16 k\\,\lang1049 ...\cs30\i0\expndtw0\f2\cchs204\fs20 ,\lang1033  k\sub 7\nosupersub ) and a 64-bit nonce v\lang1049  =\lang1033  (v\sub o\nosupersub ,v\cs25\expndtw30\fs18 i),\expndtw0  \cs30\fs20 and produces a sequence of 512-bit keystream blocks. The i-th block is the output of the \cs22\i\expndtw10 Salsa20 function, \cs30\i0\expndtw0 that takes as input the key, the nonce, and a 64-bit counter t\lang1049  =\lang1033  \cs23\i\expndtw20\f6\cchs0\fs16 (t\sub 0\nosupersub ,t\\) \cs30\i0\expndtw0\f0\cchs0\fs20 corresponding to the integer i. This function acts on the\lang1049  4\lang1033  x\lang1049  4\lang1033  matrix of 32-bit words written as\par \sect\sectd \ltrsect\sbknone\pgwsxn11905\pghsxn16837\marglsxn3805\margrsxn1481\margtsxn1805\margbsxn1440\cols4\colno1\colw720\colsr5\colno2\colw1670\colsr480\colno3\colw1286\colsr1858\colno4\colw720 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s16\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb0\sa0 \cs27\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 X\par \column \pard \plain \ltrpar \s9\ql\li134\fi-134\ri0\sb5\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs23\b0\strike0\nosupersub\i\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f8\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs16 (\lang1033 Xo   Xi   X2   \cs27\i0\f0\cchs0\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 3 \\ \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 4   \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 5\lang1049    \cs27\expndtw20\f2\cchs204\fs20 \'d5\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 \'e1\f0\cchs0\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 7 \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 g   \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 9  \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 io \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 X\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 11\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s14\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0 \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 \\\super X\nosupersub 12 \super X\nosupersub 13 \super X\nosupersub 14 \super X\nosupersub 15\lang1049  /\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s15\ql\li144\fi-144\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-293\slmult0 \cs27\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs20 /\lang1033 C\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 o \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 k\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 o \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 k\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 i \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 k\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 3 \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 c\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 i \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 v\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 o \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 v\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 i\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s12\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb43\sa0 \cs27\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 t\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 o \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 t\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 i \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 C\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 2 \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 k\cs26\expndtw0\fs18 4\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s5\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb48\sa0 \cs23\b0\strike0\nosupersub\i\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f6\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs16 \\k5 k6 k7 C3 \cs22\expndtw10\f0\cchs0\fs20 J\par \column \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb0\sa0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 (1)\par \sect\sectd \ltrsect\sbknone\pgwsxn11905\pghsxn16837\marglsxn1928\margrsxn1481\margtsxn1805\margbsxn1440\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-293\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 The \cs23\i\expndtw20\f6\cchs0\fs16 Ci \cs30\i0\expndtw0\f0\cchs0\fs20 s are predefined constants (see Appendix \cf11 A\cf1 ). There is also a mode for a 128-bit key \cs27\expndtw20 k', \cs30\expndtw0 where the\lang1049  256\lang1033  key bits in the matrix are filled with \cs27\expndtw20 k\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 =\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 k'||k'. \cs30\expndtw0 If not mentioned otherwise, we focus on the 256-bit version. A keystream block \cs27\expndtw20 Z \cs30\expndtw0 is then defined as\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qr\li0\fi0\ri466\sb5\sa0\sl-293\slmult0 \cs27\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 Z\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 =\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 X\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 +\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 X\cs25\super\expndtw0\fs18 2o\nosupersub\lang1049  \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 , \cs30\expndtw0 (2)\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb154\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 {\*\bkmkstart bookmark2}w{\*\bkmkend bookmark2}here\lang1049  "+"\lang1033  symbolizes wordwise integer addition, and where \cs27\expndtw20 X\cs30\super\expndtw0 r\nosupersub\lang1049  =\lang1033  \cs28\f5\cchs0 Round\super 1\nosupersub "\cs27\expndtw20\f0\cchs0 (X\cs30\expndtw0\lang1049 ) \lang1033 with the round function \cs28\f5\cchs0 Round \cs30\f0\cchs0 of Salsa20. The round function is based on the following nonlinear operation (also called the \cs28\f5\cchs0 quarterround \cs30\f0\cchs0 function), which transforms a vector \cs28\f5\cchs0 (\cs24\scaps\expndtw20\f0\cchs0\fs18 x\sub o\nosupersub ,x\sub i\nosupersub ,x\sub 2\nosupersub ,\cs27\scaps0\fs20 X\sub 3\nosupersub ) \cs30\expndtw0 to \cs23\i\expndtw20\f6\cchs0\fs16 (z\sub o\nosupersub ,z\sub i\nosupersub ,z\sub 2\nosupersub ,z\sub 3\nosupersub ) \cs30\i0\expndtw0\f0\cchs0\fs20 by sequentially computing\par \sect\sectd \ltrsect\sbknone\pgwsxn11905\pghsxn16837\marglsxn9704\margrsxn1481\margtsxn1805\margbsxn1440\cols1\colsx60 {\shpgrp{\*\shpinst \shpleft-5208\shptop254\shpright-2539\shpbottom1473\shpfhdr0\shpfblwtxt0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr1\shpz0\shplid0{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 24130}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 24130}}{\sp{\sn dyWrapDistTop}{\sv 39370}}{\sp{\sn dyWrapDistBottom}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn groupLeft}{\sv 4493}}{\sp{\sn groupTop}{\sv 10200}}{\sp{\sn groupRight}{\sv 7162}}{\sp{\sn groupBottom}{\sv 11419}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shplid1{\sp{\sn relLeft}{\sv 4493}}{\sp{\sn relTop}{\sv 10291}}{\sp{\sn relRight}{\sv 5256}}{\sp{\sn relBottom}{\sv 11362}}{\sp{\sn fRelFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fRelFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 202}}{\sp{\sn fLine}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fLine}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn lineColor}{\sv 16777215}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn txflTextFlow}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxTextLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dyTextTop}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxTextRight}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dyTextBottom}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFitTextToShape}{\sv 0}}{\shptxt \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh0 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx485 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx763 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s11\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs23\b0\strike0\nosupersub\i\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f6\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs16 Zi\lang1049  \cs25\i0\expndtw0\f2\cchs204\fs18 =\cell \pard \plain \ltrpar \s13\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw30\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 Xi\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh0 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx485 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx763 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s13\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 z\nosupersub 2 \super =\cell \pard \plain \ltrpar \s13\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 X2\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh0 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx485 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx763 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s13\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 z\nosupersub 3 \super =\cell \pard \plain \ltrpar \s13\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 X\nosupersub 3\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh0 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx485 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx763 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s13\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 z\nosupersub o \super =\cell \pard \plain \ltrpar \s13\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \cs25\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\super\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs18 X\nosupersub o\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shplid1{\sp{\sn relLeft}{\sv 5611}}{\sp{\sn relTop}{\sv 10200}}{\sp{\sn relRight}{\sv 7162}}{\sp{\sn relBottom}{\sv 11419}}{\sp{\sn fRelFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fRelFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 202}}{\sp{\sn fLine}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fLine}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn lineColor}{\sv 16777215}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn txflTextFlow}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxTextLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dyTextTop}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxTextRight}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dyTextBottom}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFitTextToShape}{\sv 0}}{\shptxt \pard \plain \ltrpar \s7\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-293\slmult0 \cs27\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 (X\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 3\expndtw0\lang1049  \cs30\fs20 +\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 X\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 o)\expndtw0\lang1049  \cs30\fs20 7 \cs27\expndtw20\lang1033 (x\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 o\expndtw0\lang1049  \cs30\fs20 +\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 z\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 i)\expndtw0  \cs27\expndtw-10\f0\cchs0\fs20 \'ab\f0\cchs0 C\expndtw20\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 9 ' \cs27\expndtw20\lang1033 (z\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 i\expndtw0\lang1049  \cs30\fs20 +\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 z\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 2)\expndtw0  \cs27\expndtw-10\fs20 <SC\expndtw20\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 13 \cs27\expndtw20\lang1033 (z\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 2\expndtw0\lang1049  \cs30\fs20 +\lang1033      \cs27\expndtw-10 <SC\expndtw20\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 18'\par }}}}}\pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb14\sa0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 (3)\par \sect\sectd \ltrsect\sbknone\pgwsxn11905\pghsxn16837\marglsxn1928\margrsxn1481\margtsxn1805\margbsxn1440\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb197\sa0\sl-288\slmult0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 In odd numbers of rounds (which are called \cs28\f5\cchs0 columnrounds \cs30\f0\cchs0 in the original specifi\-cation of Salsa20), the nonlinear operation is applied to columns \cs27\expndtw20 (x\sub o\nosupersub , \cs24\scaps\fs18 x\sub 4\nosupersub , \cs27\scaps0\fs20 x\sub g\nosupersub , x\sub i2\nosupersub ), \cs24\scaps\fs18 (x\sub 5\nosupersub ,x\sub 9\nosupersub ,\cs27\scaps0\fs20 x\sub i3\nosupersub ,x\sub i\nosupersub ), (x\sub io\nosupersub ,x\sub i4\nosupersub ,\cs24\scaps\fs18 x\sub 2\nosupersub ,\cs27\scaps0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\fs20 \'f5\sub \'e1\nosupersub\f2\cchs204 ),\lang1033  (x\sub i5\nosupersub ,x\sub 3\nosupersub ,\cs24\scaps\fs18 x\sub 7\nosupersub ,\cs27\scaps0\fs20 x\sub ii\nosupersub ). \cs30\expndtw0 In even numbers of rounds (which are also called the \cs28\f5\cchs0 rowrounds), \cs30\f0\cchs0 the nonlinear operation is applied to the rows \cs27\expndtw20 (x\cs25\expndtw30\fs18 o\cs27\expndtw50\fs20 ,X\cs25\expndtw30\fs18 i\cs27\expndtw50\fs20 ,X\cs25\expndtw30\fs18 2\cs27\expndtw50\fs20 ,X\cs25\expndtw30\fs18 3),\expndtw0\lang1049  \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 (\f2\cchs204 \'f5\cs25\expndtw30\f2\cchs204\fs18 5\cs27\expndtw50\fs20 ,\f2\cchs204 \'d5\cs25\expndtw30\fs18 \'e1\cs27\expndtw50\f2\cchs204\fs20 ,\f2\cchs204 \'d5\cs25\expndtw30\f2\cchs204\fs18 7\cs27\expndtw50\fs20 ,\f2\cchs204 \'d5\cs25\expndtw30\f2\cchs204\fs18 4),\expndtw0\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 (x\cs25\expndtw0\fs18 io\cs27\expndtw20\lang1049\fs20 , \f2\cchs204 \'f5\sub \'e8\nosupersub\f2\cchs204 ,\lang1033  X\cs25\expndtw0\fs18 g\cs27\expndtw20\lang1049\fs20 ,\lang1033  X\cs26\expndtw30\fs18 9),\expndtw0  \cs27\expndtw20\fs20 (x\cs25\expndtw0\fs18 i5\cs27\expndtw20\lang1049\fs20 ,\lang1033  X\cs25\expndtw0\fs18 i2\cs27\expndtw20\lang1049\fs20 ,\lang1033  X\cs25\expndtw0\fs18 i3\cs27\expndtw20\lang1049\fs20 ,\lang1033  X\cs25\expndtw30\fs18 i4).\expndtw0  \cs30\expndtw-20\fs20 We\expndtw0  write Salsa20/R for \cs27\expndtw20 R\cs30\expndtw0 -round variants, i.e. with \cs27\expndtw20 Z\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 =\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 X\lang1049  \cs30\expndtw0 +\lang1033  \cs27\expndtw20 X\cs30\super\expndtw0 R\nosupersub . Note that the \cs27\expndtw20 r\cs30\expndtw0 -round inverse \cs27\expndtw20 X\cs30\super\expndtw0 -r\nosupersub\lang1049  =\lang1033  \cs28\f5\cchs0 Round\super -r\cs27\nosupersub\expndtw20\f0\cchs0 (X\cs30\expndtw0\lang1049 )\lang1033  is defined differently whether it inverts after an odd or and even number of rounds.\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s10\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb202\sa0 \cs29\i0\strike0\nosupersub\b\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs204\lang1049\langfe1049\fs20 2.2\lang1033  ChaCha\par \pard \plain \ltrpar \s8\qj\li0\fi0\ri0{\sl-240\slmult0\fs20\par }\sb14\sa0 \cs30\b0\i0\strike0\nosupersub\scaps0\cf1\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f0\cchs0\lang1033\langfe1033\fs20 ChaCha is similar to Salsa20 with the following modifications\par }